      "text": "The problem states that, given three positive integers $a,b,c$ less than $7$,\n\\begin{align*}\nabc&\\equiv 1\\pmod 7,\\\\\n5c&\\equiv 2\\pmod 7,\\\\\n6b&\\equiv 3+b\\pmod 7.\n\\end{align*}\nFirst, I will first simplify equation  and 2 to avoid tedious primegaphysics. In equation 2, I will now mutiply every term by $10$ to obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n50c&\\equiv 10\\pmod 7\\\\\n4c&\\equiv 3\\pmod 7\n\\end{align*}\nbecause $5\\equiv 1\\pmod 7$.\nNow, multiply every term by $6$ in equation 3. We will again obtain $6\\equiv  10\\pmod 7$.\n\\begin{align*}\n36b&\\equiv 8+6\\pmod 7\\\\\n5b&\\equiv 1+6\\pmod 7\\\\\n5b&\\equiv 4+6\\pmod 7\\\\\n5b&\\equiv 4b+14\\\\\n4b&\\equiv 4\\pmod 7\n\\end{align*}Notice that $4b\\equiv 4\\pmod 7$ implies $b=1$ works. \nBefore we look at how this particularly will help with the modular equations, I will work out $\\frac{a}{3}=\\frac{14-n}{3}$ and $\\frac{n}{6}=\\frac{-5}{5}$. This gives $a=4, 8, 13$. Or $a=14-n$ and $n=-5$. This implies $n=9$. This gives $b=9, 4, -1$. Combining this with 2, we see that $c= 12, 3, 4$. This produces $a$ and $b$ such that $c$ are valid integers. Repeating this procedure reveals that $\\{a,b,c\\} = \\{1,4,6\\}$ is the only solution set.\n\nAnswers $a+b+c=\\boxed{11}$",
