      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start by solving the third congruence equation. This can be rearranged to $\\text{ }b\\equiv 3+b-5c\\pmod 7\\implies b\\equiv 3-2c\\pmod 7$. Then we plug the answer obtained from the third congruence into the second congruence. This gives $\\text{ }5c\\equiv 2\\pmod 7$. Rearranging this equation gives us $c\\equiv 3\\pmod7$. Next we use this value for $c$ in the first congruence to find the remainder when $a+b+c$ is divided by $7$. This gives $\\text{ }abc\\equiv 1\\pmod7$. In order to simplify this, we write $a\\equiv ac\\pmod 7$ and $c\\equiv 3c\\pmod7$. This then means $a\\equiv 3ac\\pmod 7$. This means $3ac\\equiv 1\\pmod7$. She should be very careful with modular arithmetic since one can have \"-1 ≡ 6(mod7)\". By performing modular arithmetic (3ac ≡ 1 ≡ -1 modulo 7 ) this now means that $ac≈6$ modulo 7. Any combination we plug into this system of equations should give us a solution. Plugging $a\\equiv 3, c\\equiv 3$ back into $abc≡1 \\pmod7$ gives us $abc\\equiv 27 ≡ 6\\pmod7$. This gives us the solution of $a\\equiv3, b\\equiv3, c\\equiv3$. The remainder when we divide $a+b+c$ by $7$ is $3+3+3$ or 9. We divide $9\\pmod7$ to get 2. Therefore, the final answer is boxed as $\\boxed2.$\n\nThe is one of those problems where you chain together all the modular multiplication equations, unscramble them, and you end up with just addition.",
