      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Since $5c\\equiv 2\\pmod7$, we see that $c\\equiv 4\\pmod7$. Since $6b\\equiv 3\\pmod7$, we see that $b\\equiv 2\\pmod7$. But we also have $6b\\equiv 3+b\\pmod7$ (since $6b\\equiv b+b+2b\\equiv 3+b+b+3\\equiv b+3\\pmod7$ and $2+3\\equiv 5\\pmod7$). So $b\\equiv 2\\pmod7$. Let's now plug these back in our first equation, giving $a\\equiv 4\\pmod7$, so that $a=4$.\n\nThus $b+c\\equiv 2+4\\equiv 6\\pmod 7$.\n\nTherefore, $a+b+c\\equiv 4+2+6\\equiv \\boxed{12}\\pmod7\\boxed{6}$. The answer is 12",
